    <section class="swp-content">
      <h2>What is an SWP (Systematic Withdrawal Plan) Calculator?</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP) Calculator</strong> is a specialized financial modeling tool designed to help mutual fund investors plan regular, periodic cash outflows from their accumulated corpus. Unlike a lump-sum redemption, an SWP allows you to withdraw a fixed or inflation-indexed amount of money on a monthly basis while the remainder of your investment continues to stay invested and generate returns.
      </p>

      <h2>How Does an SWP Calculator Work?</h2>
      <p>
        The mechanics of an SWP involve monthly compounding returns balanced against periodic redemptions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Growth Applied First:</strong> In our calculation engine, each month begins with your starting balance earning investment returns at the monthly rate: <code>Monthly Rate = Annual Return ÷ 12 ÷ 100</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Scheduled Withdrawal:</strong> After applying monthly compounding growth, the pre-determined monthly withdrawal amount is deducted from the portfolio.</li>
        <li><strong>Corpus Longevity:</strong> If the returns generated by your investments exceed your monthly withdrawal, your corpus continues to grow or sustain indefinitely. If withdrawals exceed growth, the principal gradually decreases over time until the corpus is depleted.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Core Calculation Methodology &amp; Depletion Handling</h2>
      <p>
        The step-by-step mathematical algorithm executed by this calculator follows this exact standard:
      </p>
      <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 text-xs sm:text-sm space-y-1.5 font-mono">
        <div>1. Monthly Return Rate = Annual Return Rate ÷ 1200</div>
        <div>2. For each month m = 1 to (Years × 12):</div>
        <div class="pl-4">a. Balance = Balance × (1 + Monthly Return Rate)</div>
        <div class="pl-4">b. Year Number = ceil(m ÷ 12)</div>
        <div class="pl-4">c. Scheduled Withdrawal = Initial Withdrawal × (1 + Inflation Rate)^(Year Number - 1)</div>
        <div class="pl-4">d. If Balance ≥ Scheduled Withdrawal: Balance = Balance - Scheduled Withdrawal</div>
        <div class="pl-4">e. Else: Total Withdrawn += Balance; Balance = 0; Corpus Depleted; Break.</div>
      </div>

      <h2>SWP Calculator with Inflation: Protecting Purchasing Power</h2>
      <p>
        A flat withdrawal of ₹25,000 per month may comfortably support your lifestyle today, but over a 10, 15, or 20-year horizon, persistent inflation reduces real purchasing power. With a 6% annual inflation rate, an expense that costs ₹25,000 in Year 1 will require:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Year 1:</strong> ₹25,000 / month</li>
        <li><strong>Year 2:</strong> ₹26,500 / month (+6%)</li>
        <li><strong>Year 5:</strong> ₹31,562 / month (+26.2%)</li>
        <li><strong>Year 10:</strong> ₹42,237 / month (+68.9%)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The <strong>SWP Calculator with Inflation</strong> mode allows retirees and financial planners to factor in this yearly step-up to verify whether the initial corpus can support escalating living expenses over the entire retirement horizon.
      </p>

      <h2>Fixed Withdrawal vs. Inflation-Adjusted Withdrawal</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 text-slate-900 dark:text-white font-bold">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Feature</th>
              <th class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Simple SWP (Fixed)</th>
              <th class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">SWP with Inflation</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 dark:divide-slate-800 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Monthly Payout</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Constant throughout the tenure</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Increases once every 12 months by inflation rate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Purchasing Power</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Gradually declines due to cost of living</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Preserved against general inflation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Corpus Longevity</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Lasts longer due to constant outflow</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Requires higher initial capital or return rate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Ideal For</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Supplementary income &amp; loan repayments</td>
              <td class="p-3 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">Full retirement living expense planning</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Beginning of Month vs. End of Month Withdrawal Timing</h2>
      <p>
        The timing of your monthly redemptions affects how long your capital stays invested and generates returns:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Beginning of Month (Default):</strong> The scheduled withdrawal is deducted at the start of each month, and investment growth is calculated on the remaining balance. This accurately reflects scenarios where you need funds on the 1st of every month for living expenses, rent, or EMIs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>End of Month:</strong> The full balance earns investment growth across the month first, and the withdrawal is deducted at the end of the month. Because money remains invested for an additional month before deduction, the final corpus is slightly higher.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Can this Calculator be used for SBI Mutual Fund SWP?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. This calculator is a completely independent, general-purpose financial tool that can be used to simulate SWP scenarios for various mutual fund houses across India, including <strong>SBI Mutual Fund</strong> schemes (such as SBI Bluechip Fund, SBI Equity Hybrid Fund, SBI Balanced Advantage Fund, or debt funds), as well as schemes from HDFC, ICICI Prudential, Nippon India, and others.
      </p>
      <p>
        Simply input your initial investment amount, desired monthly cash flow, and your expected conservative rate of return based on the asset allocation of your chosen mutual fund scheme.
      </p>

      <h2>Important Assumptions &amp; Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Constant Return Assumption:</strong> The calculator assumes a steady annualized rate of return compounded monthly. Actual mutual fund returns fluctuate daily depending on market conditions.</li>
        <li><strong>Taxation:</strong> SWP redemptions are subject to Capital Gains Tax (LTCG / STCG based on holding period and equity vs debt classification). Net returns received in your bank account will reflect applicable tax deductions.</li>
        <li><strong>Exit Loads:</strong> If redemptions occur within the scheme's exit load window (typically within 1 year for equity funds), an exit load of 1% may apply to redemptions exceeding the scheme's free limit.</li>
      </ul>

